{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 argues that sky view factor is the 'single dominant cross-city modulator' of nocturnal UHI magnitude, but it conflates SVF's radiative-trapping role with the stored-thermal-mass effect it is supposed to be modulating. The mechanism text says low SVF 'slows the nocturnal cooling rate, so the stored thermal mass effect persists longer' — i.e., SVF's influence is mediated through how long stored heat persists, not through the amount of heat stored or released. On a clear, calm night the heat island is driven by the absolute release of stored sensible heat, which depends on thermal admittance and building materials; if one city has much higher thermal mass (and thus releases more stored heat) but only moderately higher SVF, SVF-based radiative trapping cannot account for the larger gap. The target's own counterconditions concede this: it offers no principled reason why thermal mass differences across cities are always subordinate to SVF differences, yet it asserts SVF as the single best modulator. For the specific problem — which asks for the single mechanism best explaining why the night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others — the target over-privileges a geometry variable without establishing that radiative trapping dominates over heat-storage capacity as a cross-city control.","premise":"The cross-city magnitude of nocturnal UHI depends on the total stored heat released overnight, which is governed primarily by urban thermal admittance/heat capacity; SVF only modulates the cooling rate of whatever heat is present.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"5c58a1e361650cc390675bad","quote":"Urban materials with high thermal mass and heat capacity store daytime insolation and release it as sensible heat after sunset. In dense street canyons with low sky view factor, emitted longwave radiation has a high probability of intercepting another building facade rather than escaping to the cold sky."}],"counterexample":[{"block":"5c58a1e361650cc390675bad","quote":"Two cities with identical SVF but one built of high-admittance concrete and the other of low-admittance wood: the concrete city releases more stored heat overnight and develops a larger nocturnal UHI, contradicting SVF as the single dominant modulator."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}